.TH WINLESS 1 "November 2026" "winless 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
winless \- a less-style viewer in its own window, drivable from the shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B winless
[\fB\-\-port\fR \fIN\fR]
\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B winless
shows a text file in a chromeless browser window (full screen by default) with the keys of
.BR less (1),
and lets scripts drive it. Under WSL the window opens on the Windows desktop (Microsoft Edge or Chrome).
A small built-in HTTP server (one thread, started on demand; default port 8777); the viewer page receives commands
from it over Server-Sent Events and posts its state and key presses back, so a program can also read where the
person is reading. Right-to-left text such as Hebrew is laid out with the browser's bidirectional text support.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI open " " [FILE|\-]
Load a file, or standard input, and open the window if none is connected. Options:
.BR \-\-name ,
.BR \-\-line " N,"
.BR \-\-search " PATTERN,"
.BR \-\-theme " dark|light,"
.BR \-\-font " FAMILY,"
.BR \-\-size " PX,"
.BR \-N " (line numbers),"
.BR \-S " (chop long lines),"
.BR \-\-windowed " (normal window instead of full screen),"
.BR \-\-no\-window " (load only),"
.BR \-\-wait " (block until q or the window closes)."
.TP
.BI goto " LINE|N%|top|bottom"
Jump to a line, a percentage, or an end.
.TP
.BR scroll " [\-p] " N
Scroll N lines (pages with \-p); negative scrolls up.
.TP
.BR search " [\-b] " PATTERN
Search (smart-case regular expression) and jump to the first match. \fBnext\fR and \fBprev\fR step; \fBclear\fR removes it.
.TP
.BR mark " A[\-B] [" COLOR ]
Shade a line range; \fBunmark\fR removes all shading.
.TP
.BR hl " " PATTERN " [" COLOR ]
Highlight a regular expression wherever it occurs; \fBunhl\fR removes them.
.TP
.BI key " KEY..."
Send keys as if typed (j k space b g G / n and the names enter, up, down, pgup, pgdn, home, end, esc).
.TP
.BR config " [\-\-theme T] [\-\-font F] [\-\-size N] [\-\-numbers|\-\-no\-numbers] [\-\-wrap|\-\-chop]"
Change the display.
.TP
.BI flash " TEXT"
Show a short message in the window.
.TP
.BR status " [\-\-json] [\-\-text]"
Report the visible line range, search state and window size; \-\-text also prints the visible lines.
.TP
.B wait
Block until q is pressed or the window is closed.
.TP
.BR window ", " close ", " start ", " stop ", " serve
Open the window; close it; start the server in the background; stop it; run it in the foreground.
.TP
.B edit R1 C1 R2 C2 TEXT|\-
Replace the range from row R1, column C1 up to (not including) row R2, column C2 with TEXT, or with standard input
for \-. Rows count from 1; columns count Unicode code points from 1; column (length + 1) is the end of a row. The change
is made in the server's copy of the text (undoable, shown with [+]); the file is not touched until
.BR write .
.TP
.B undo
Undo the last edit.
.TP
.B annotate [\-\-kind note|slop|keep] R1 C1 R2 C2 [NOTE]
.TQ
.B annotate [\-\-kind K] \-\-file [NOTE]
Add a note on a range (rows and columns from 1, columns in code points, the end excluded, as for
.BR edit ),
or on the whole file. See ANNOTATIONS.
.TP
.B revise [\-\-backend B] [\-\-id N | \-\-range R1 C1 R2 C2] [\-\-wait]
Ask a model to address the open notes (all, note N, or those inside a range). With \-\-wait, wait for it and print the proposed changes.
.TP
.B review status | accept N | reject N | accept\-all | reject\-all | edit N TEXT | finish | cancel
Look at, decide, or finish the current review.
.TP
.B backend claude|api
Choose the model backend for this server.
.TP
.B annotations [\-\-all] [\-\-deleted]
List the notes on this file (or on every file); \-\-deleted includes hidden ones.
.TP
.B unannotate [\-\-restore] ID
Hide a note, or with \-\-restore bring it back. Nothing is ever removed from the database.
.TP
.B filter R1 C1 R2 C2 [\-\-lines] [\-\-timeout S] COMMAND
Replace the range with the output of a shell command run on it. See FILTERS.
.TP
.B history
List the earlier versions of the file that writes have kept, oldest first.
.TP
.B revert [N] [\-\-force]
Put back version N of the file (default: the latest kept) and write it. A revert is a write, so it keeps what it replaces and can
itself be reverted. Refused while there are unsaved edits, unless \-\-force.
.TP
.B redo
Redo the last undone edit.
.TP
.B ex COMMAND
Run an ex command in the window as if typed after a colon: s/a/b/g, %s/a/b/, 2,5s/a/b/, w, 42 ...
.TP
.B type [\-\-commit] TEXT
Fill the bottom panel (opened by c, i, a, o ...) with TEXT, and with \-\-commit apply it.
.TP
.B write [\-f]
Write the text to the file it was opened from, atomically (a temporary file in the same directory, then a rename).
It refuses when the file has changed on disk since it was opened; \-f overwrites anyway.
.SH ANNOTATIONS
In visual mode
.B n
opens the bottom panel for a note on the selection (Ctrl-Enter saves, Esc cancels).
Notes are kept in a SQLite database, ~/.local/share/winless/winless.db, one row each: the file, the date and time (UTC), the row and
column where the text begins and ends, what it covers, the selected text, and the note. A selection inside one paragraph (one line)
is stored with its text (scope
.BR text );
a selection over several paragraphs stores only which paragraphs (scope
.BR paragraphs ,
for example 3-4); a selection of everything, or
.B :annotate TEXT
or
.BR "annotate \-\-file" ,
is a note on the whole file (scope
.BR file ,
no position). Notes on text are underlined where they were made, as long as the text there is still what was selected;
notes on paragraphs put a mark in the margin.
.B :annotations
lists them, ]a and [a jump to the next and previous (wrapping around), and
.B :unannotate N
hides one (:unannotate! N brings it back). Notes are never deleted.
.SH FILTERS
.B :range!command
replaces the range with the output of the shell command, run on that text, in the directory of the file (the file's path is in
$WINLESS_FILE). It is one undo step, and if the command fails (a non-zero exit, or longer than 30 seconds) nothing changes and the
error is shown. Ranges: % . $ N,M and \(aq<,\(aq> (the selected lines, as in vim); \(aq<> is exactly the selection, to the character.
In visual mode ! offers :\(aq<,\(aq>! (line selection) or :\(aq<>! (character selection); !! offers :.! for the current line. A filter
needs a range, so a mistyped :!command cannot overwrite anything.
.PP
Because a filter runs shell commands, the server only answers requests from pages on this machine: a request with a Host or Origin
that is not localhost or 127.0.0.1 on its own port (another website, or a rebound hostname) is refused.
.SH REVISING WITH A MODEL
Mark text while you read, then hand all the marks to a model at once. In visual mode
.B n
makes a note (an instruction in your own words),
.B s
marks slop (writing of the kind you do not want; the words on why are optional) and
.B K
marks text to keep untouched. Then
.B gr
asks the model to address this file's open notes (in visual mode, only those inside the selection;
.B :revise N
just note N). The model is not asked to rewrite anything: it answers with a list of edits, each naming the exact text to find, what
to put instead, and why. winless checks every edit against the real text: the text must occur exactly once and must not overlap
text marked keep or another edit, or it is listed as one that could not be applied (never guessed at;
.B P
shows them). The changes are shown in place, old text struck on red and new text on green with the changed words picked out, and
the bar says which note caused each one. Nothing is applied yet. In the review,
.B n
and
.B N
move to the next and previous change, wrapping around;
.B y
accepts and
.B q
rejects the current one and moves on;
.B c
lets you type your own wording (and accepts it);
.B Y
accepts all;
.B Enter
finishes and
.B Esc
cancels. Finishing applies the accepted changes as one undoable edit, marks the notes they answered done, and, if the text had no
unsaved edits, writes the file (the write history keeps the old one) and commits it with git, but only if the file was in a
repository with no uncommitted changes beforehand, so your own edits are never swept into a commit. Otherwise the result is left unsaved
and winless says why. The review is recorded in the reviews table of the database. A revision is refused if the text changed while the
model was working.
.PP
The model is run in the background, so the window stays usable;
.B Esc
cancels. The backend is
.B claude
(the default: `claude -p` with no tools and no saved session, so it can only answer) or
.B api
(not built yet);
.B :set backend
or
.B winless backend
switches it, and the environment variables below set the default.
.SH REVERTING
Every write keeps what it is about to replace, in ~/.local/share/winless/history/ (one directory per file, one numbered snapshot per
write). A write whose old content is identical to the new writes nothing extra, and if the old content cannot be kept the write is
refused. So any change that reached a file can be undone with
.B winless revert
(or :revert [N] in the window; :history lists the versions), even after the window is gone, even if the write overwrote someone
else's change with :w!. Nothing is pruned. Edits that were never written live only in the window and are discarded when it quits.
.SH WINDOWS
Each
.B open
starts its own server, on a port chosen at that moment, and opens a window for it, so the server serves just that one window and
opening a window never disturbs the others. A file that is already open is not opened twice: winless says which port has it
(\-\-new opens another anyway). \-\-here replaces the text of the one existing window instead (refused while it has unsaved edits,
unless \-\-force). The servers record their ports in ~/.cache/winless/ports; a server on the default port 8777 is found as well.
.B winless windows
lists them with the file, whether a window is connected, and [unsaved] when there are edits not yet written.
.PP
When more than one window is open, a command that acts on one (goto, key, status, edit, write ...) does not guess: on a terminal
it shows the list and asks which; otherwise it prints the list and exits with status 2. \-\-port N (or $WINLESS_PORT) picks one.
With a single window no question is asked. A server started for a window ends with that window, as quitting vim does: when the
window quits (:q!, :wq or q) it ends at once, and a minute after the window is simply closed. Edits that were not written are
discarded then; closing a window that has unsaved edits asks first, and :q refuses until you write with :w (or :wq, which writes
and quits) or discard with :q!.
.B stop \-\-all
stops every server, and (like
.B stop
and
.BR "open \-\-here" )
refuses one that has unsaved edits unless \-\-force.
.SH REMOTE HOSTS
.B winless ssh
.RI [ ssh-options ]
.I host
.RI [ command ]
(also installed as
.BR sshw )
is ssh with winless working on the host: inside it,
.B winless open FILE
shows the window on this desktop while the server, the file, history, git and filters stay on the host. For example:
.PP
.nf
    sshw myserver                       # a shell on myserver, as ssh gives
    myserver$ winless open notes.txt    # the window appears on this desktop
    myserver$ winless goto 120          # driven from the host's shell
.fi
.PP
A host that has no winless gets a static build uploaded on first use. See
.BR sshw (1)
for the options, the requirements and what to do when something does not work.
.SH KEYS
j, Down, Enter, e: line down; k, Up, y: line up; Space, f, PageDown: page down; b, PageUp: page up; d, u: half page;
g, Home / G, End: top / bottom; /pattern, ?pattern: search forward/backward; n, N: next/previous match, wrapping around at the ends of the file (like vim's wrapscan); h, Left / l, Right: scroll sideways when long lines are chopped (w toggles), 0: back to column 0; Ctrl-d, Ctrl-u: half page; Ctrl-f, Ctrl-b: page; Ctrl-e, Ctrl-y: line; gg: top;
:NUMBER, :N%: go to; # or \-N: line numbers; w or \-S: wrap/chop; t: theme; Ctrl-plus/minus: font size; F1 or :help: help; q: quit.
.SH CURSOR, VISUAL MODE AND EDITING
The window has a cursor, always shown as a steady block. The keys are vim's: h, j, k, l and the arrow keys move it (j and k by screen
row, so a wrapped paragraph is walked row by row), w, b, e, 0, $, ( ), { }, G and gg move it further, and a mouse click puts it
there. The page scrolls to keep it in view, and Space, PageUp, PageDown, Ctrl-f, Ctrl-b, Ctrl-d, Ctrl-u, Ctrl-e and Ctrl-y scroll
(the cursor follows). A search puts the cursor on the match.
.PP
v starts a character selection at the cursor, V a line selection, and dragging with the mouse a character selection. Every motion
extends it; o swaps its ends; Esc leaves; gv reselects. y yanks, d deletes, c opens a text panel at the bottom with the selection
(Ctrl-Enter applies, Esc cancels), and p replaces the selection with the register. In normal mode d, c and y take a motion or a text
object: dw, cw, d$, dd, cc, yy, ciw, cis, dip, and x, D, C, Y work too. p pastes after the cursor, and u undoes the last edit (when
there is nothing to undo, u is half a page up; Ctrl-r redoes). i, a, I, A, o and O open the same panel to type new text (Ctrl-Enter
inserts it before or after the cursor, at the start or end of the line, or on a new line below or above). r replaces characters, J joins
lines, and counts work as in vim (3j, 2w, 3x, 2dd, d2w, 5G). :s/pattern/replacement/[gi] substitutes, with the ranges %, ., $, N,M and
\(aq<,\(aq> (the selected lines); \(aq<> means exactly the selected text. The pattern is a JavaScript regular expression; & and \\1 work in
the replacement. :w, :w!, :q, :q! and :wq write and quit as in vim; q refuses while there are unsaved
changes. Edits live in the server until written, and a write that would overwrite a file changed by someone else is refused.
Open a file (not standard input) to edit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WINLESS_BACKEND
The model backend for revisions: claude (default) or api. Read by the server when it starts.
.TP
.B WINLESS_MODEL
The model name passed to the backend (claude: \-\-model).
.TP
.B WINLESS_EFFORT
The effort level passed to claude (\-\-effort).
.TP
.B WINLESS_LLM
Run this shell command instead of a backend: the request as JSON ({"system", "user", "model"}) on its standard input, the reply on
standard output. For testing, or another provider.
.TP
.B WINLESS_LLM_TIMEOUT
Seconds to wait for the model (default 900).
.TP
.B WINLESS_CLAUDE
The claude executable (default: claude, from PATH).
.TP
.B WINLESS_PORT
Server port. 
.TP
.B WINLESS_BROWSER
Path of the browser executable used for the window.
.TP
.B WINLESS_HTML
Alternate viewer page.
.TP
.B WINLESS_PROFILE
Windows path of the browser profile directory used for the window (default: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\winless\\profile). A dedicated profile makes the browser start a separate process, which is what makes the full-screen and window-size flags take effect when Edge is already running. Set it to the empty string to use the browser's normal profile.
.TP
.B WINLESS_BELL
Set inside
.BR "winless ssh" :
the socket to ring to have this desktop open the window. See
.BR sshw (1).
.TP
.B WINLESS_SSH
The ssh program used by
.B winless ssh
(default: ssh).
.TP
.B WINLESS_REMOTE_DIR
The archive of binaries that sshw uploads to hosts that have no winless (DIR/OS\-MACHINE/winless), used instead of
~/.local/share/winless/remote; builds made on a host are kept there.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.cache/winless/server.log
Server log.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/winless/ports/PORT
A running server: its process id, and its secret (mode 0600; every request but /ping must carry it).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sshw (1),
.BR less (1)
.SH AUTHOR
David Walther.
